--TEST--
A write to a `readonly` property through an `object` receiver or `Core\Reflect\ClassInfo::set` throws, and the value stays as the constructor wrote it — `rule:classes/lateinit-restrictions`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The checker refuses every `readonly` write it can see. These two writes name
// no class it could check, so the run-time check throws instead.
class Box {
    public readonly int $v;
    public int $other = 0;
    public function constructor(int $v) {
        $this->v = $v;
    }
}

var $box = new Box(1);

// An `object` receiver has no class to check the write against.
object $erased = $box;
try {
    $erased->v = 9;
    echo "erased write", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// A reflective write throws the same error.
var $described = Core\Reflect::forObject($box);
try {
    $described->set($box, "v", 9);
    echo "reflective write", "\n";
} catch (RuntimeError $refused) {
    echo $refused->message, "\n";
}

// A property that is not `readonly` is still written.
$erased->other = 5;
echo $box->v, " ", $box->other, "\n";
--EXPECT--
cannot modify `readonly` property `Box::$v`
cannot modify `readonly` property `Box::$v`
1 5
